WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.247
Part one built every power

2
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:07.504
from X lambda X inverse.

3
00:00:07.504 --> 00:00:09.490
That needs one thing

4
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.378
enough independent eigenvectors

5
00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:16.260
to make the grid, one for every dimension.

6
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.435
Distinct eigenvalues always give enough.

7
00:00:19.435 --> 00:00:22.049
If two eigenvectors lay on one line

8
00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:24.380
A would stretch that line by two

9
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.287
different numbers at once

10
00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:27.770
which is impossible.

11
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.446
But what if an eigenvalue repeats?

12
00:00:31.446 --> 00:00:33.697
Is the grid then lost?

13
00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:35.360
Pause and think.

14
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.102
A common guess

15
00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:42.579
a repeated eigenvalue means the

16
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:44.980
matrix cannot be diagonalized.

17
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.010
The identity has eigenvalue one, twice.

18
00:00:49.098 --> 00:00:51.133
Stretch it to one and a

19
00:00:51.221 --> 00:00:53.167
half times I and back

20
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:56.263
every arrow stays on its own line.

21
00:00:56.263 --> 00:00:59.359
So every vector is an eigenvector

22
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.720
and the identity is diagonal already.

23
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.632
The shear one, one, zero

24
00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:09.321
one also has eigenvalue one, twice.

25
00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:13.624
But apply it: the horizontal arrow stays

26
00:01:13.624 --> 00:01:17.928
and every other arrow leans further over

27
00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:19.670
turn after turn.

28
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.804
Only one line of eigenvectors, so no grid.

29
00:01:23.804 --> 00:01:27.769
Its powers have a corner that grows like k

30
00:01:27.769 --> 00:01:31.373
even though the only eigenvalue is one.

31
00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:34.977
A repeated eigenvalue may fail, or not

32
00:01:34.977 --> 00:01:37.140
count the eigenvectors.

33
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.387
Now where powers matter.

34
00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:43.610
A sequence starts zero, one

35
00:01:43.722 --> 00:01:47.723
and each term is twice the last plus

36
00:01:47.834 --> 00:01:52.168
three times the one before: two, seven

37
00:01:52.168 --> 00:01:54.280
twenty, sixty-one.

38
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.486
Keep two neighbouring terms in a vector.

39
00:01:58.486 --> 00:02:02.825
One step is a matrix: two, three, one, zero.

40
00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:06.778
Its eigenvalues are three and minus one

41
00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:09.863
so each term is three to the k

42
00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:13.430
minus minus one to the k, over four.

43
00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.678
Look at the direction of the vector

44
00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:17.805
step by step.

45
00:02:17.805 --> 00:02:19.093
It starts flat

46
00:02:19.093 --> 00:02:21.991
jumps above the line of three, one

47
00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:24.084
falls below, rises above

48
00:02:24.084 --> 00:02:26.580
and closes in from both sides.

49
00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.873
The eigenvalue three wins

50
00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:31.637
so the ratio of terms goes to three.

51
00:02:31.637 --> 00:02:34.850
The minus one only makes the vector swing.

52
00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.921
A population of young and adult animals.

53
00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:42.412
Each grown animal has six young a year

54
00:02:42.412 --> 00:02:44.506
half the young grow up

55
00:02:44.506 --> 00:02:47.910
and half of the grown animals survive.

56
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.046
The eigenvalues are two and

57
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.317
minus one and a half

58
00:02:53.317 --> 00:02:57.445
with eigenvectors three, one, and four

59
00:02:57.445 --> 00:02:58.580
minus one.

60
00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.070
Start with ten adults and no young.

61
00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:05.209
The next year is almost all young.

62
00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:06.824
Then almost even.

63
00:03:06.824 --> 00:03:09.067
Then mostly young again.

64
00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:10.771
The swings shrink

65
00:03:10.771 --> 00:03:14.090
each one three quarters of the last.

66
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.677
The minus one and a half flips

67
00:03:16.752 --> 00:03:18.243
its part every year

68
00:03:18.243 --> 00:03:21.152
but loses ground to the doubling part.

69
00:03:21.152 --> 00:03:23.986
In the long run: doubling every year

70
00:03:23.986 --> 00:03:26.148
three young for every adult

71
00:03:26.148 --> 00:03:27.640
whatever the start.

72
00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:31.218
A linear recurrent network applies the

73
00:03:31.296 --> 00:03:33.742
same matrix at every time step.

74
00:03:33.742 --> 00:03:36.108
Here are two that look alike

75
00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:39.185
zero point eight and zero point three

76
00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:42.340
or zero point seven and zero point two.

77
00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.565
The first has largest eigenvalue

78
00:03:45.654 --> 00:03:46.912
one point one.

79
00:03:46.912 --> 00:03:49.966
One step, a second step, a third

80
00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:53.649
the state creeps outward along one, one.

81
00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:56.973
After fifty steps it is eighty-three

82
00:03:57.063 --> 00:03:58.410
times the size.

83
00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.933
The second has largest eigenvalue

84
00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:03.568
zero point nine.

85
00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:06.837
One step, a second step, a third

86
00:04:06.837 --> 00:04:09.241
the state creeps inward.

87
00:04:09.241 --> 00:04:13.280
After fifty steps almost nothing is left.

88
00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:16.737
Training through many steps multiplies

89
00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:19.190
gradients by the same powers.

90
00:04:19.272 --> 00:04:22.133
That is the exploding and vanishing

91
00:04:22.215 --> 00:04:23.605
gradient problem

92
00:04:23.605 --> 00:04:26.630
and it is decided by one eigenvalue.

93
00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.824
Last, a made-up assistant answer

94
00:04:29.906 --> 00:04:33.173
for the gold in part one: four to the k

95
00:04:33.173 --> 00:04:34.970
plus two, over three.

96
00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.936
At the start it gives one, like the loop.

97
00:04:38.936 --> 00:04:41.493
One turn later it gives two

98
00:04:41.493 --> 00:04:43.345
the loop says three.

99
00:04:43.345 --> 00:04:46.343
The two coefficients are swapped

100
00:04:46.343 --> 00:04:48.900
and four to the zero is one

101
00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.840
which hides the swap.

102
00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.248
A closed form is a claim about every k.

103
00:04:54.248 --> 00:04:57.462
Check it against a loop for many values

104
00:04:57.462 --> 00:04:59.030
not just the first.

105
00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.770
So, to sum up

106
00:05:00.852 --> 00:05:04.331
repeating a matrix pulls every vector onto

107
00:05:04.413 --> 00:05:07.312
the line of its biggest eigenvalue.

108
00:05:07.395 --> 00:05:09.631
A negative eigenvalue makes

109
00:05:09.714 --> 00:05:11.370
it swing on the way.

110
00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.675
Bigger than one in size, it grows; smaller

111
00:05:15.675 --> 00:05:16.594
it fades.

112
00:05:16.594 --> 00:05:20.365
And all of it needs enough eigenvectors

113
00:05:20.365 --> 00:05:22.480
the shear has too few.

114
00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.372
Next lecture, time flows continuously.

115
00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:30.119
Powers lambda to the k become exponentials

116
00:05:30.206 --> 00:05:31.775
e to the lambda t

117
00:05:31.775 --> 00:05:35.697
and the same eigenvectors decide what grows

118
00:05:35.697 --> 00:05:38.660
what decays, and what oscillates.
